  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nDry conditions are expected through Black Friday as a low pressure\nsystem and subsequent precipitation stay well off to the east. The\nnext chance for precipitation will be Friday night into Saturday\nmorning as a weak cold front track through the western Great Lakes\nregion. Although this system will lack deep moisture and lift,\nthere is enough moisture in the lower levels (up to ~10k ft) where\nlight rain or drizzle is likely to fall. This looks to be a\nclassic high POP/low QPF event where precipitation will likely\nfall (60-90% chance) but the amount that falls will likely be very\nsmall, on the order of a few tenths of an inch. Model soundings\nindicate precipitation would mainly be rain across central and\neast-central Wisconsin, with freezing rain across northern\nWisconsin given the dearth of deep level moisture and substantial\nwarm layer at 850 mb. Even with minor amounts expected, any\nfreezing rain or drizzle could be impactful on untreated roadways.\n\nA low pressure system passing through the Ohio Valley will bring\nthe next chance for precipitation late Saturday night into Sunday\nwith a possible glancing blow across central, east-central, and\nnortheast Wisconsin. Precipitation chances will remain low \n(10-30%) as the best moisture and lift will be well to the \nsoutheast. Precipitation type south mainly be rain across east-\ncentral, with some snow possibly mixing in across central and\nnortheast Wisconsin. However, given the lack of substantial\nmoisture and lift any snow that fell would be very minor. At this\npoint would not be shocked if this system shifted a bit southeast\nand miss the area entirely with QPF as some of the ensembles\nsuggest.\n\nDry weather then rules for the early part of next week as a ridge\nof high pressure tracks through the Great Lakes region. A couple\nof low pressure systems could bring chances for precipitation\nduring the middle part of next week. These systems will mainly be\nnorthern stream systems and will lack deep moisture. Therefore,\nprecipitation chances during this period are low (10-30%).\nPrecipitation type would mainly be rain across the south, with\nsnow or a rain/snow mix across the north. Again, due to the lack\nof substantial moisture, any snowfall amounts would be on the\nlighter side with minimal impact.\n\nTemperatures...High temperatures will be about 5-10 degrees above\nnormal through Saturday, with values mainly in the 40s. \nTemperatures will take a dip near normal for Sunday and Monday \nwith the passage of the late weekend systems, but will rebound \nback above normal for Tuesday through next Thursday.",
